          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The declarative avowal is the expression’s dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The avowal is explicitly framed as love owed under Cordelia’s existing filial bond. This invokes, rather than newly creates, her duties as Lear’s daughter; her subsequent explanation identifies obedience, love, and honour as those duties. Lear does not accept this as an adequate declaration.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured declarative answer, carefully limited by Cordelia’s account of her bond. Although the situation is emotionally charged, the words are not represented as a reflex-like or uncontrolled discharge of affect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The expression also functions as a principled refusal to participate in Lear’s competitive, hyperbolic love test. Cordelia limits her claim to what she regards as truthful and fitting rather than trying to maximize her reward.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Nothing, my lord.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_1\",\"quotation\":\"So young, my lord, and true.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most and links their answers to the division of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia is ordered to speak again. She avows love for Lear but restricts it to the love and duty appropriate to their father–daughter bond, rejecting the expansive rhetoric used by her sisters.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing filial obligation: as Lear’s daughter, she owes him fitting love, obedience, honour, care, and duty. She does not clearly make a new promise in the focal words. Lear treats the answer as deficient, urges her to revise it, and then repudiates her, so there is no affirmative uptake of her formulation.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text identifies the work as William Shakespeare’s The Tragedy of King Lear; the focal speaker is Cordelia addressing Lear in Act I, Scene I.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied passages strongly support Cordelia’s represented affection through her asides, insistence on truth, grief, and later care for Lear. They do not provide access to an independent psychological state beyond the literary representation, and the precise legal or customary terms of her filial “bond” are not fully specified.\"}"
        }
